# health v2.9 — the coded allergy record, whole.
#
# WHAT THIS FIXTURE IS FOR. Through health v2.8 an allergy record could name its
# substance and its manifestations only as display text, so a converter reading
# a FHIR AllergyIntolerance had nowhere to put code.coding or
# reaction.manifestation.coding and dropped both. The IPS AllergyIntolerance
# profile makes AllergyIntolerance.code 1..1 must-support and marks
# reaction.manifestation must-support, so a record that carries neither cannot
# be exported into an International Patient Summary at all.
#
# This record carries every element of that repair at once, because the point is
# that the four facts coexist on one node rather than that each parses alone:
#
#   health:allergenCode        one RxNorm IRI, beside the display text in
#                              health:allergen. RxNorm rather than SNOMED CT on
#                              purpose: the vocabulary imposes no lexical
#                              pattern precisely so that RxNorm, SNOMED CT and
#                              UNII identifiers, which share no format, are all
#                              writable. A fixture that only ever used SNOMED
#                              would not exercise that.
#   health:manifestationCode   TWO SNOMED CT IRIs, with TWO matching
#                              health:reaction literals, ONE PER MANIFESTATION.
#                              This is the shape of the data the vocabulary now
#                              says is correct, against the joined
#                              comma-separated literal a converter used to emit;
#                              nothing here would fail if the two were joined,
#                              which is exactly why the correct form is written
#                              down as a fixture rather than left to a comment.
#   health:allergySeverity     "severe" — what the OBSERVED reaction was
#                              (AllergyIntolerance.reaction.severity).
#   clinical:criticality       "high" — the risk a FUTURE exposure carries
#                              (AllergyIntolerance.criticality).
#
# THE LAST TWO ARE THE LOAD-BEARING PAIR. They are different value sets on
# different axes and they sit on the record together here for the same reason
# the sibling status fixture carries clinical:status and
# clinical:verificationStatus together: to show that the two are separate
# elements and not one fact spelled twice. A converter that folds criticality
# into severity when no reaction severity is present destroys both, because a
# reader given one stored value cannot afterwards tell which question it
# answers. This record answers both.
#
# clinical:allergyType "allergy" carries AllergyIntolerance.type. Both
# clinical: predicates are written on a health:AllergyRecord, which is what
# clinical v1.18 dropping their rdfs:domain clinical:Allergy is for.
#
# Fully synthetic. No such patient exists.

@prefix cascade: <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/core/v1#> .
@prefix clinical: <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/clinical/v1#> .
@prefix health: <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/health/v1#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<urn:uuid:a11e0209-0000-4000-8000-000000000001> a health:AllergyRecord ;
    health:allergen "Penicillin G" ;
    health:allergenCode <http://www.nlm.nih.gov/research/umls/rxnorm/7980> ;
    health:allergyCategory "medication" ;
    health:reaction "Hives" ;
    health:reaction "Dyspnea" ;
    health:manifestationCode <http://snomed.info/sct/247472004> ;
    health:manifestationCode <http://snomed.info/sct/267036007> ;
    health:allergySeverity "severe" ;
    clinical:criticality "high" ;
    clinical:allergyType "allergy" ;
    clinical:status "active" ;
    clinical:verificationStatus "confirmed" ;
    clinical:sourceEHR "Meridian Health System" ;
    cascade:dataProvenance cascade:EHRVerified ;
    cascade:schemaVersion "1.4" .
